         (100, 'C'), (90, 'XC'), (50, 'L'), (40, 'XL'),\n            (10, 'X'), (9, 'IX'), (5, 'V'), (4, 'IV'), (1, 'I')\n        ]\n\n    def get_mappings(self) -> list[tuple[int, str]]:\n        return self._mappings\nclass RomanNumeralConverter:\n    \"\"\"Converts a validated NumberInput to a RomanNumeral.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.mapping_rules = MappingRules()\n\n    def convert(self, number_input: NumberInput) -> RomanNumeral:\n        num = number_input.value\n        roman_parts = []\n        mappings = self.mapping_rules.get_mappings()\n\n        for value, symbol in mappings:\n            while num >= value:\n                roman_parts.append(symbol)\n                num -= value\n        \n        return RomanNumeral(\"\".join(roman_parts).lower())\ndef int_to_mini_roman(number):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer, obtain its roman numeral equivalent as a string,\n    and return it in lowercase.\n    Restrictions: 1 <= num <= 1000\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(19) == 'xix'\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(152) == 'clii'\n    >>> int_to_mini_roman(426) == 'cdxxvi'\n    \"\"\"\n    number_input = NumberInput(number)\n    \n    # The Validator's validate method will always return True per the prompt's constraints.\n    # Error handling (e.g., raising ValueError) is explicitly disallowed by the prompt.\n    if Validator().validate(number_input):\n        roman_converter = RomanNumeralConverter()\n        roman = roman_converter.convert(number_input)\n        return roman.value", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/158": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/158", "solution": "def find_max(words):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that accepts a list of strings.\n    The list contains different words. Return the word with maximum number\n    of unique characters. If multiple strings have maximum number of unique\n    characters, return the one which comes first in lexicographical order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not words:\n        return \"\"\n\n    max_word = words[0]\n    max_unique_count = len(set(words[0]))\n\n    for i in range(1, len(words)):\n        current_word = words[i]\n        current_unique_count = len(set(current_word))\n\n        if current_unique_count > max_unique_count:\n            max_unique_count = current_unique_count\n            max_word = current_word\n        elif current_unique_count == max_unique_count:\n            # If unique counts are equal, use lexicographical order (smaller string)\n            if current_word < max_word:\n                max_word = current_word\n    \n    return max_word", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/159": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/159", "solution": "class Result:\n    \"\"\"Holds the final output pair and provides a method to serialize it as an ordered list.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, total_eaten: int, remaining_after: int):\n        self.total_eaten = total_eaten\n        self.remaining_after = remaining_after\n\n    def to_list(self) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the result as an ordered list [total_eaten, remaining_after].\"\"\"\n        return [self.total_eaten, self.remaining_after]\nclass CarrotStock:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the remaining carrot stock and logic for consuming carrots.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, remaining: int):\n        self.remaining = remaining\n\n    def consume(self, amount: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the actual amount of carrots consumed, limited by remaining stock.\"\"\"\n        consumed_amount = min(amount, self.remaining)\n        return consumed_amount\nclass RabbitState:\n    \"\"\"Manages the rabbit's total number of eaten carrots.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, totalEaten: int):\n        self.totalEaten = totalEaten\n\n    def update(self, eaten: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Adds a given number of carrots to the total eaten.\"\"\"\n        self.totalEaten += eaten\nclass EatFunction:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the core business logic for the carrot eating process.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, number: int, need: int, remaining: int):\n        self.number = number\n        self.need = need\n        self.remaining = remaining\n\n    def execute(self) -> Result:\n        \"\"\"Performs the calculations for carrots eaten and remaining after the meal.\"\"\"\n        carrot_stock = CarrotStock(self.remaining)\n        carrots_to_eat = carrot_stock.consume(self.need)\n\n        rabbit_state = RabbitState(self.number)\n        rabbit_state.update(carrots_to_eat)\n        \n        total_eaten = rabbit_state.totalEaten\n        remaining_after = self.remaining - carrots_to_eat\n        \n        return Result(total_eaten, remaining_after)\ndef eat(number, need, remaining):\n    \"\"\"\n    You're a hungry rabbit, and you already have eaten a certain number of carrots,\n    but now you need to eat more carrots to complete the day's meals.\n    you should return an array of [ total number of eaten carrots after your meals,\n                                    the number of carrots left after your meals ]\n    if there are not enough remaining carrots, you will eat all remaining carrots, but will still be hungry.\n    \n    Example:\n    * eat(5, 6, 10) -> [11, 4]\n    * eat(4, 8, 9) -> [12, 1]\n    * eat(1, 10, 10) -> [11, 0]\n    * eat(2, 11, 5) -> [7, 0]\n    \n    Variables:\n    @number : integer\n        the number of carrots that you have eaten.\n    @need : integer\n        the number of carrots that you need to eat.\n    @remaining : integer\n        the number of remaining carrots thet exist in stock\n    \n    Constrain:\n    * 0 <= number <= 1000\n    * 0 <= need <= 1000\n    * 0 <= remaining <= 1000\n\n    Have fun :)\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_inputs(number, need, remaining)\n    \n    eat_function_instance = EatFunction(number, need, remaining)\n    result = eat_function_instance.execute()\n    \n    return result.to_list()", "base_status": "fail", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [[5, 6, 10]], "plus_fail_tests": [[0, 0, 0]]}], "HumanEval/162": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/162", "solution": "import hashlib\nfrom typing import Optional\ndef string_to_md5(text: str) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string 'text', return its md5 hash equivalent string.\n    If 'text' is an empty string, return None.\n\n    Encoding decision: text is encoded to UTF-8 bytes prior to hashing to ensure deterministic output.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not text:\n        return None\n    return hashlib.md5(text.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/157": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/157", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import Any\nclass NumericValidator:\n    \"\"\"Utility for validating numeric inputs.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_positive_finite(value: Any) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns True if value is numeric (int or float), > 0, not NaN, and finite.\n        Returns False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n            return False\n        if isinstance(value, bool): # Explicitly exclude booleans as side lengths\n            return False\n        if not math.isfinite(value) or math.isnan(value):\n            return False\n        return value > 0\nclass Triangle:\n    \"\"\"Domain container for triangle sides and triangle-specific checks.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a: float, b: float, c: float) -> None:\n        self.a = a\n        self.b = b\n        self.c = c\n\n    def satisfies_triangle_inequality(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns True if the sum of any two sides is greater than the third side,\n        False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        return (self.a + self.b > self.c and\n                self.a + self.c > self.b and\n                self.b + self.c > self.a)\nclass RightAngleChecker:\n    \"\"\"Implements Pythagorean comparison using configured tolerance and selects hypotenuse.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, tolerance: float = 1e-9) -> None:\n        self.tolerance = tolerance\n\n    def is_right_angle(self, triangle: Triangle) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Determines if the given triangle is right-angled using the Pythagorean theorem\n        and a specified tolerance.\n        \"\"\"\n        sides = sorted([triangle.a, triangle.b, triangle.c])\n        leg1, leg2, hypotenuse = sides[0], sides[1], sides[2]\n\n        return abs(hypotenuse**2 - (leg1**2 + leg2**2)) <= self.tolerance\ndef right_angle_triangle(a: Any, b: Any, c: Any) -> bool:\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return True if the three\n    sides form a right-angled triangle, False otherwise.\n    A right-angled triangle is a triangle in which one angle is right angle or \n    90 degree.\n    Example:\n    right_angle_triangle(3, 4, 5) == True\n    right_angle_triangle(1, 2, 3) == False\n    '''\n    if not NumericValidator.is_positive_finite(a):\n        return False\n    if not NumericValidator.is_positive_finite(b):\n        return False\n    if not NumericValidator.is_positive_finite(c):\n        return False\n\n    float_a, float_b, float_c = float(a), float(b), float(c)\n\n    triangle = Triangle(float_a, float_b, float_c)\n    if not triangle.satisfies_triangle_inequality():\n        return False\n\n    checker = RightAngleChecker(tolerance=1e-9)\n    return checker.is_right_angle(triangle)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/155": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/155", "solution": "class IntegerInput:\n    \"\"\"Represents the input integer for processing.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n\n    def is_valid(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the stored value is an integer.\"\"\"\n        return isinstance(self.value, int)\nclass Digit:\n    \"\"\"Represents a single decimal digit and its parity.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n        self.is_even = (value % 2 == 0)\nclass CountResult:\n    \"\"\"Stores the aggregated counts of even and odd digits.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, even_count: int, odd_count: int) -> None:\n        self.even_count = even_count\n        self.odd_count = odd_count\n\n    def as_tuple(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the counts as a (even_count, odd_count) tuple.\"\"\"\n        return (self.even_count, self.odd_count)\nclass DigitCounter:\n    \"\"\"Counts even and odd digits from an IntegerInput.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.even_count = 0\n        self.odd_count = 0\n\n    def classify_digit(self, digit_value: int) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Classifies a single digit as 'even' or 'odd'.\"\"\"\n        return 'even' if digit_value % 2 == 0 else 'odd'\n\n    def count_digits(self, integer_input: 'IntegerInput') -> 'CountResult':\n        \"\"\"Processes the input integer to count its even and odd digits.\"\"\"\n        abs_value = abs(integer_input.value)\n\n        if abs_value == 0:\n            # Handle the case of input 0 specifically (0 is even)\n            digit_obj = Digit(0)\n            if digit_obj.is_even:\n                self.even_count += 1\n            return CountResult(self.even_count, self.odd_count)\n\n        temp_value = abs_value\n        while temp_value > 0:\n            digit_value = temp_value % 10\n            digit_obj = Digit(digit_value)\n            if digit_obj.is_even:\n                self.even_count += 1\n            else:\n                self.odd_count += 1\n            temp_value //= 10\n        \n        return CountResult(self.even_count, self.odd_count)\ndef even_odd_count(num):\n    \"\"\"Given an integer. return a tuple that has the number of even and odd digits respectively.\n\n     Example:\n        even_odd_count(-12) ==> (1, 1)\n        even_odd_count(123) ==> (1, 2)\n    \"\"\"\n    input_obj = IntegerInput(num)\n    # As per design, behavior for invalid input (is_valid() returns False) is undefined.\n    # The design specifies to `pass` if not valid.\n    if not input_obj.is_valid():\n        pass \n\n    counter = DigitCounter()\n    result = counter.count_digits(input_obj)\n    return result.as_tuple()", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/160": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/160", "solution": "import operator as op_module\nfrom typing import List, Union\nclass OperandList:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates operand list and validation logic (non-negative integer checks).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, operands: list) -> None:\n        self.operands = operands\n\n    def validate_non_negative(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates that each operand is a non-negative integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.operands, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Operand list must be a list.\")\n        if len(self.operands) < 2:\n            raise ValueError(\"Operand list must have at least two operands.\")\n        for item in self.operands:\n            if not isinstance(item, int) or item < 0:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Operand '{item}' must be a non-negative integer.\")\nclass OperatorList:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates operator list and validation logic (allowed operators).\"\"\"\n    ALLOWED_OPERATORS = {'+', '-', '*', '//', '**'}\n\n    def __init__(self, operators: list) -> None:\n        self.operators = operators\n\n    def validate_allowed(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates that each operator is in the allowed set.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.operators, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Operator list must be a list.\")\n        if len(self.operators) < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Operator list must have at least one operator.\")\n        for item in self.operators:\n            if not isinstance(item, str) or item not in self.ALLOWED_OPERATORS:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Operator '{item}' is not allowed. Must be one of {self.ALLOWED_OPERATORS}.\")\nclass Expression:\n    \"\"\"Builds the token sequence in the exact alternating pattern.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        pass\n\n    def construct(self, operands: list, operators: list) -> List[Union[int, str]]:\n        \"\"\"Constructs the token sequence in alternating pattern: operand[0], operator[0], operand[1], ...\"\"\"\n        tokens: List[Union[int, str]] = []\n        for i in range(len(operators)):\n            tokens.append(operands[i])\n            tokens.append(operators[i])\n        tokens.append(operands[len(operators)]) # Add the last operand\n        return tokens\nclass Evaluator:\n    \"\"\"Evaluates tokenized expressions applying precedence and associativity; implements floor-division semantics and raises ZeroDivisionError for division by zero.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        pass\n\n    def _perform_op(self, operand1: int, operator: str, operand2: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Performs a single algebraic operation.\"\"\"\n        if operator == '+':\n            return operand1 + operand2\n        elif operator == '-':\n            return operand1 - operand2\n        elif operator == '*':\n            return operand1 * operand2\n        elif operator == '//':\n            if operand2 == 0:\n                raise ZeroDivisionError(\"division by zero\")\n            return operand1 // operand2\n        elif operator == '**':\n            return operand1 ** operand2\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unknown operator: {operator}\")\n\n    def _reduce_by_precedence_level(self, tokens: List[Union[int, str]], operators_to_process: set, right_associative: bool = False) -> List[Union[int, str]]:\n        \"\"\"Applies a specific precedence level of operators to the token list.\"\"\"\n        current_tokens = list(tokens)\n\n        if right_associative:\n            i = len(current_tokens) - 2\n            while i >= 0:\n                if isinstance(current_tokens[i], str) and current_tokens[i] in operators_to_process:\n                    operand2 = current_tokens.pop(i + 1)\n                    operator = current_tokens.pop(i)\n                    operand1 = current_tokens.pop(i - 1)\n                    \n                    result = self._perform_op(operand1, operator, operand2)\n                    current_tokens.insert(i - 1, result)\n                    i -= 2\n                i -= 1\n            return current_tokens\n        else:\n            i = 1\n            while i < len(current_tokens):\n                if isinstance(current_tokens[i], str) and current_tokens[i] in operators_to_process:\n                    operand1 = current_tokens.pop(i - 1)\n                    operator = current_tokens.pop(i - 1)\n                    operand2 = current_tokens.pop(i - 1)\n                    \n                    result = self._perform_op(operand1, operator, operand2)\n                    current_tokens.insert(i - 1, result)\n                else:\n                    i += 2\n            return current_tokens\n\n    def apply_precedence(self, tokens: List[Union[int, str]]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Orchestrates evaluation by applying operators based on their precedence.\"\"\"\n        current_tokens = list(tokens)\n\n        current_tokens = self._reduce_by_precedence_level(current_tokens, {'**'}, right_associative=True)\n        current_tokens = self._reduce_by_precedence_level(current_tokens, {'*', '//'})\n        current_tokens = self._reduce_by_precedence_level(current_tokens, {'+', '-'})\n\n        if len(current_tokens) != 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Expression evaluation failed: result is not a single number.\")\n\n        return int(current_tokens[0])\n\n    def evaluate(self, tokens: List[Union[int, str]]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Evaluates the tokenized expression according to operator precedence and associativity.\"\"\"\n        return self.apply_precedence(tokens)\ndef do_algebra(operator: list, operand: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two lists operator, and operand. The first list has basic algebra operations, and \n    the second list is a list of integers. Use the two given lists to build the algebric \n    expression and return the evaluation of this expression.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(operator, list) or not isinstance(operand, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Inputs 'operator' and 'operand' must be lists.\")\n    if len(operator) != len(operand) - 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Length of operator list must be equal to length of operand list minus one.\")\n\n    operand_list_obj = OperandList(operand)\n    operand_list_obj.validate_non_negative()\n\n    operator_list_obj = OperatorList(operator)\n    operator_list_obj.validate_allowed()\n\n    expr_builder = Expression()\n    tokens = expr_builder.construct(operand_list_obj.operands, operator_list_obj.operators)\n\n    evaluator = Evaluator()\n    result = evaluator.evaluate(tokens)\n\n    return result", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": [[["-", "+", "**", "**"], [5, 3, 3, 2, 2]]]}], "HumanEval/161": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/161", "solution": "class InputString:\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self._value = value\n\n    def length(self) -> int:\n        return len(self._value)\n\n    def containsLetters(self) -> bool:\n        for char_value in self._value:\n            # Position is not relevant for this check\n            if Character(0, char_value).isLetter():\n                return True\n        return False\n\n    def charAt(self, pos: int) -> 'Character':\n        return Character(pos, self._value[pos])\nclass Character:\n    def __init__(self, position: int, value: str):\n        self.position = position\n        self.value = value\n\n    def isLetter(self) -> bool:\n        return self.value.isalpha()\n\n    def invertCase(self) -> str:\n        if self.isLetter():\n            return self.value.swapcase()\n        return self.value\nclass ResultString:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._chars = []\n\n    def append(self, c: str):\n        self._chars.append(c)\n\n    def toString(self) -> str:\n        return \"\".join(self._chars)\nclass Solver:\n    def solve(self, s: str) -> str:\n        if not s:\n            return \"\"\n\n        input_string = InputString(s)\n\n        if not input_string.containsLetters():\n            return s[::-1]\n        else:\n            result_string = ResultString()\n            for i in range(input_string.length()):\n                char_obj = input_string.charAt(i)\n                if char_obj.isLetter():\n                    result_string.append(char_obj.invertCase())\n                else:\n                    result_string.append(char_obj.value)\n            return result_string.toString()\ndef solve(s):\n    \"\"\"You are given a string s.\n    if s[i] is a letter, reverse its case from lower to upper or vise versa, \n    otherwise keep it as it is.\n    If the string contains no letters, reverse the string.\n    The function should return the resulted string.\n    Examples\n    solve(\"1234\") = \"4321\"\n    solve(\"ab\") = \"AB\"\n    solve(\"#a@C\") = \"#A@c\"\n    \"\"\"\n    return Solver().solve(s)", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/94": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/94", "solution": "import math\ndef skjkasdkd(lst):\n    \"\"\"You are given a list of integers.\n    You need to find the largest prime value and return the sum of its digits.\n    If no prime numbers are found, 0 is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class Integer:\n        \"\"\"Represent a single integer value and provide primitive operations.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, value: int):\n            self.value = value\n\n        def isPrime(self) -> bool:\n            \"\"\"Determine whether self.value is prime as per definition.\"\"\"\n            if self.value <= 1:\n                return False\n            if self.value == 2:\n                return True\n            if self.value % 2 == 0:\n                return False\n            for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(self.value)) + 1, 2):\n                if self.value % i == 0:\n                    return False\n            return True\n\n        def digitSum(self) -> int:\n            \"\"\"Compute and return the sum of decimal digits of self.value.\"\"\"\n            return sum(int(digit) for digit in str(self.value))\n\n    class PrimeNumber(Integer):\n        \"\"\"Domain type representing an integer known to be prime.\"\"\"\n        # Inherits __init__, isPrime, and digitSum from Integer.\n        pass\n\n    class IntegerList:\n        \"\"\"Container for integers; provides methods to identify and select primes.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.integers: list[Integer] = []\n\n        def add(self, value: int) -> None:\n            \"\"\"Add a new Integer for the given value.\"\"\"\n            self.integers.append(Integer(value))\n\n        def getPrimes(self) -> list[PrimeNumber]:\n            \"\"\"Return a list of PrimeNumber instances that satisfy isPrime().\"\"\"\n            primes = []\n            for integer_obj in self.integers:\n                if integer_obj.isPrime():\n                    primes.append(PrimeNumber(integer_obj.value))\n            return primes\n\n        def getLargestPrime(self) -> PrimeNumber | None:\n            \"\"\"Return the largest PrimeNumber instance, or None if none exist.\"\"\"\n            all_primes = self.getPrimes()\n            if not all_primes:\n                return None\n            \n            largest_prime_obj = None\n            for prime_obj in all_primes:\n                if largest_prime_obj is None or prime_obj.value > largest_prime_obj.value:\n                    largest_prime_obj = prime_obj\n            return largest_prime_obj\n\n    class Result:\n        \"\"\"Represent the computed result (digit sum) and provide conversion to primitive integer.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, digitSum: int):\n            self.digitSum = digitSum\n\n        def toInt(self) -> int:\n            \"\"\"Return digitSum as a plain int.\"\"\"\n            return self.digitSum\n\n    integer_list_container = IntegerList()\n    for item in lst:\n        integer_list_container.add(item)\n\n    largest_prime = integer_list_container.getLargestPrime()\n\n    if largest_prime:\n        digit_sum_value = largest_prime.digitSum()\n        result_obj = Result(digit_sum_value)\n        return result_obj.toInt()\n    else:\n        # Returns 0 if no primes are found, per design context.\n        return 0", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/83": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "def starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        return 18 * (10**(n - 2))", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/130": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef tri(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Computes the first n + 1 numbers of the Tribonacci sequence.\n    The sequence is defined by specific rules for even and odd indices.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        # Per instructions, do not add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions.\n        # However, a negative n does not make sense for \"first n+1 numbers\".\n        # Based on \"non-negative integer n\" in problem, assuming n >= 0.\n        return []\n    \n    if n == 0:\n        return [1]\n    \n    # Initialize the list to store the sequence values\n    values = [0] * (n + 1)\n    \n    # Base cases\n    values[0] = 1\n    values[1] = 3\n    \n    # Compute subsequent values\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            # Even rule: tri(n) = 1 + n / 2\n            values[i] = 1 + (i // 2)\n        else:\n            # Odd rule: tri(n) = tri(n - 1) + tri(n - 2) + tri(n + 1)\n            # tri(n + 1) is computed using the even rule\n            tri_next_even_val = 1 + ((i + 1) // 2)\n            values[i] = values[i - 1] + values[i - 2] + tri_next_even_val\n            \n    return values", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/139": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "class Input:\n    def __init__(self, n: int) -> None:\n        self.n = n\nclass Factorial:\n    def __init__(self, k: int) -> None:\n        self.k = k\n        self.value = 0 \n\n    def compute(self) -> int:\n        # Computes k! iteratively and stores it.\n        if self.k == 0:\n            self.value = 1\n        else:\n            product = 1\n            for i in range(1, self.k + 1):\n                product *= i\n            self.value = product\n        return self.value\nclass BrazilianFactorialCalculator:\n    def __init__(self, n: int) -> None:\n        self.n = n\n        self.result = 1 \n\n    def compute(self) -> int:\n        # Aggregates k! for k from 1 to n into the Brazilian factorial.\n        current_brazilian_factorial = 1\n        for k_val in range(1, self.n + 1):\n            factorial_instance = Factorial(k_val)\n            k_factorial = factorial_instance.compute()\n            current_brazilian_factorial *= k_factorial\n        self.result = current_brazilian_factorial\n        return self.result\nclass Result:\n    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None:\n        self.value = value\ndef special_factorial(n):\n    \"\"\"The Brazilian factorial is defined as:\n    brazilian_factorial(n) = n! * (n-1)! * (n-2)! * ... * 1!\n    where n > 0\n\n    For example:\n    >>> special_factorial(4)\n    288\n\n    The function will receive an integer as input and should return the special\n    factorial of this integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    input_obj = Input(n)\n    calculator = BrazilianFactorialCalculator(input_obj.n)\n    computed_result = calculator.compute()\n    result_obj = Result(computed_result)\n    return result_obj.value", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/36": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/36", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass Input:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates and validates the input parameter n.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, n: int) -> None:\n        self.n = n\n\n    def validate(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Validates that n is an integer; raises ValueError if not.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(self.n, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"Input n must be an integer.\")\nclass IntegerValue:\n    \"\"\"Represents an integer and provides divisibility and digit counting capabilities.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: int) -> None:\n        self.value = value\n\n    def isDivisibleBy11(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the integer value is divisible by 11.\"\"\"\n        return self.value % 11 == 0\n\n    def isDivisibleBy13(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the integer value is divisible by 13.\"\"\"\n        return self.value % 13 == 0\n\n    def countDigitSeven(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Counts occurrences of the digit '7' in the absolute decimal representation.\"\"\"\n        return str(abs(self.value)).count('7')\nclass IntegerSet:\n    \"\"\"Manages a collection of IntegerValue objects and provides filtering by divisibility.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, input_obj: Input) -> None:\n        if input_obj.n <= 0:\n            self.values: List[IntegerValue] = []\n        else:\n            self.values = [IntegerValue(i) for i in range(input_obj.n)]\n\n    def filterDivisibleBy11Or13(self) -> List[IntegerValue]:\n        \"\"\"Returns a list of IntegerValue objects divisible by 11 or 13.\"\"\"\n        return [iv for iv in self.values if iv.isDivisibleBy11() or iv.isDivisibleBy13()]\nclass Counter:\n    \"\"\"Aggregates the count of the digit '7' from a filtered set of integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.totalSevens: int = 0\n\n    def countSevens(self, integer_set: IntegerSet) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates and returns the total count of digit '7' in the relevant integers.\"\"\"\n        for iv in integer_set.filterDivisibleBy11Or13():\n            self.totalSevens += iv.countDigitSeven()\n        return self.totalSevens\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"A value object to hold the final count of digit '7' occurrences.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, totalSevens: int) -> None:\n        self.totalSevens = totalSevens\ndef fizz_buzz(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return the number of times the digit 7 appears in integers less than n which are divisible by 11 or 13.\n    >>> fizz_buzz(50)\n    0\n    >>> fizz_buzz(78)\n    2\n    >>> fizz_buzz(79)\n    3\n    \"\"\"\n    input_obj = Input(n)\n    input_obj.validate()\n    integer_set = IntegerSet(input_obj)\n    counter = Counter()\n    total_sevens = counter.countSevens(integer_set)\n    result = Result(total_sevens)\n    return result.totalSevens", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "HumanEval/163": [{"task_id": "HumanEval/163", "solution": "from typing import List, Set\nclass IntegerRange:\n    \"\"\"Computes inclusive integer range boundaries and enumerates values in [start..end].\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, start: int, end: int) -> None:\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n\n    def computeRange(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns a list of integers from start to end, inclusive.\"\"\"\n        return list(range(self.start, self.end + 1))\nclass EvenDigitsSet:\n    \"\"\"Represents the fixed candidate set {2,4,6,8} and filters input lists to that set.\"\"\"\n    digits: Set[int] = {2, 4, 6, 8}\n\n    def filter(self, values: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns values that are also present in the fixed set of even digits.\"\"\"\n        return [val for val in values if val in self.digits]\nclass ResultList:\n    \"\"\"Holds selected integer values and provides sorting.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, values: List[int]) -> None:\n        self.values = values\n\n    def sortAsc(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Sorts the stored values in ascending order and returns the sorted list.\"\"\"\n        self.values.sort()\n        return self.values\nclass GenerateIntegersFunction:\n    \"\"\"Orchestrates computation: compute range, filter candidates, produce sorted result.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a: int, b: int) -> None:\n        self.a = a\n        self.b = b\n\n    def execute(self) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"Executes the integer generation process and returns the sorted even digits.\"\"\"\n        range_start = min(self.a, self.b)\n        range_end = max(self.a, self.b)\n        \n        integer_range = IntegerRange(start=range_start, end=range_end)\n        range_values = integer_range.computeRange()\n\n        even_digits_set = EvenDigitsSet()\n        matched_values = even_digits_set.filter(range_values)\n\n        result_list = ResultList(matched_values)\n        return result_list.sortAsc()\ndef generate_integers(a, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given two positive integers a and b, return the even digits between a\n    and b, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    facade = GenerateIntegersFunction(a, b)\n    return facade.execute()", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "fail", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": [[987654321, 123456789]]}]}}